\n   552\t## Admin endpoint isolation (BE-0357)\n   553\t\n   554\t`admin_endpoi
nt_isolation_static_test.go` pins the operator-facing\n   555\tcontract that the
customer-facing API listener ships NO `/debug/*`\n   556\tsurface — no pprof, no
expvar, no `golang.org/x/net/trace`, no\n   557\tdefault-mux fallback. Four real
-file matchers run alongside one\n   558\tsynthetic self-check\n   559\t(`TestAd
minEndpointIsolationStaticAnalyzerDetectsRegressions`):\n   560\t\n   561\t- **`
cmd/yalla-api/main.go` + `internal/controlplane/httpapi/*.go`\n   562\t  (non-te
st files)** — `findForbiddenAdminImports` walks the AST\'s\n   563\t  import dec
larations and rejects any path in\n   564\t  `forbiddenAdminImports` (`net/http/
pprof`, `expvar`,\n   565\t  `golang.org/x/net/trace`). The match is path-based,
so blank\n   566\t  imports (`_ "net/http/pprof"`), named imports\n   567\t  (`p
prof "net/http/pprof"`), and aliased imports\n   568\t  (`rogue "net/http/pprof"
`) are all caught — the side-effect\n   569\t  registration fires regardless of 
the local alias. Test files\n   570\t  (`*_test.go`) are deliberately skipped vi
a `adminProductionFiles`\n   571\t  so a future runtime regression test can simu
late the threat\n   572\t  without tripping the gate.\n   573\t- **`cmd/yalla-ap
i/main.go`** — `findAdminHandlerRegressions`\n   574\t  locates every `http.Serv
er` composite literal (via\n   575\t  `isAdminHTTPServerCompositeLit`, the packa
ge-qualified\n   576\t  `http.Server` selector) and rejects: (1) the absence of 
the\n   577\t  `Handler` key entirely (the Go zero value falls back to\n   578\t
 `http.DefaultServeMux`), and (2) `Handler: nil` explicitly (same\n   579\t  fal
lback, written down). The matcher targets only `http.Server`\n   580\t  literals
so unrelated structs in main.go are untouched.\n   581\t- **`internal/controlpla
ne/httpapi/server.go`** —\n   582\t  `findAdminPrivateMuxRegressions` runs two o
rthogonal passes\n   583\t  against the handler file. The first asserts a call t
o\n   584\t  `http.NewServeMux()` appears somewhere in the file; the absence\n  
585\t  means the gate cannot prove the handler is built on a private\n   586\t  
mux. The second walks every SelectorExpr and rejects any whose\n   587\t  `Sel.N
ame` equals `DefaultServeMux` — this catches\n   588\t  `http.DefaultServeMux.Ha
ndle("…", …)`, passing\n   589\t  `http.DefaultServeMux` as an argument, or retu
rning it from a\n   590\t  function. Both passes are required because a regressi
on that\n   591\t  builds a private mux AND ALSO hands routes to the default mux
\n   592\t  would slip past either pass in isolation.\n   593\t- **`cmd/yalla-ap
i/main.go` + `internal/controlplane/httpapi/*.go`\n   594\t  (non-test files)** 
— `findAdminDebugLiteralRegressions` runs the\n   595\t  belt-and-braces literal
scan. The string-literal pass rejects any\n   596\t  literal containing a member
of `forbiddenAdminLiterals`\n   597\t  (`/debug/pprof`, `/debug/vars`, `/debug/r
equests`, `/debug/events`,\n   598\t  `http.DefaultServeMux`); the selector pass
rejects any\n   599\t  SelectorExpr whose root identifier is in\n   600\t  `forb
iddenAdminSelectorPackages` (`pprof`, `expvar`). The literal\n   601\t  scan is 
case-sensitive (the stdlib paths are case-sensitive at\n   602\t  the HTTP layer
); the selector scan catches an unaliased import\n   603\t  use (`pprof.Index`, 
`expvar.Handler`) even when the import\n   604\t  scanner would have missed a di
fferent import path.\n   605\t- **`SECURITY.md`** —\n   606\t  `TestAdminEndpoin
tIsolationSecurityDocumented` pins the\n   607\t  `## Admin Endpoint Isolation` 
